4754	    /// `reconnect_after_exec`) hold the scheduler lock but run on whichever
4755	    /// backend worker fielded the RPC, not on the scheduler daemon's turn. The
4756	    /// point in the daemon's loop at which such a handler acquires the lock is
4757	    /// host-timing-dependent on asynchronous backends (e.g. DBT): it may land
4758	    /// inside the tentative-pop window (between `step3_peek` and `step4`'s
4759	    /// commit, where the lock is released across `req.get().await`) *or* outside
4760	    /// it (during the quiescence-wait / backoff awaits at the top of
4761	    /// `do_a_turn_blocking`, where `tentative_selection` is `None`). A design
4762	    /// that pushed directly whenever the window happened to be closed would make
4763	    /// the *admission order* — and, under `RunsPostFork::Random`, the PRNG draw
4764	    /// order — a function of that host timing: two equal-priority admissions
4765	    /// could enter the queue in either relative order across otherwise-identical
4766	    /// runs, and a fixed seed could explore different schedules.
4767	    ///
4768	    /// So admission is *always* deferred, never applied directly here. Handlers
4769	    /// only record the unresolved [`AdmitIntent`]; the daemon resolves the side
4770	    /// (drawing any `RunsPostFork::Random` value) and pushes the run queue at the
4771	    /// single `step2` drain, in canonical `DetTid` order, before `step3` opens a
4772	    /// tentative window. Draining is also the only place `remove_tid`'s tentative
4773	    /// guard is guaranteed to hold.
4774	    ///
4775	    /// # What this does and does not make deterministic
4776	    ///
4777	    /// **Synchronous backends (ptrace): fully deterministic, and byte-identical
4778	    /// to the pre-deferral behavior.** Handlers run post-commit, one per turn, so
4779	    /// at most one admission is buffered per turn and it drains at the next
4780	    /// `step2` — before that turn's `step3` selection — yielding the same
4781	    /// selection sequence and the same one-draw-per-fork PRNG order as an
4782	    /// immediate push.
4783	    ///
4784	    /// **Asynchronous backends: every production admission site has a causal or
4785	    /// explicit barrier that fixes its drain, and order within that drain is
4786	    /// canonical.** A bare off-turn handler would still be insufficient: a
4787	    /// `BTreeMap` only canonicalizes items already in one snapshot. The current
4788	    /// sites additionally bind snapshot membership to deterministic scheduler
